A `ready` receipt gives the entry's path (`entry`), in its `summary` the
`ARCHS` and `CELLS` as committed, and the card's command you watched pass as
its `check`.

When the defect is in shared code outside the entry (an upstream kernel, a
library the entry wraps, the engine) and the checkpoint's own call fails on
it, write `blocked` as soon as you have measured that. Keep the guard and
the failing test on your branch, and name the defect by what triggers it,
not by this model: the symbol, the parameter regime that hits it, the
failing test as `command` with its `result` line, and the fix you propose as
`next_question`.

When the operation is more than one kernel call and nothing better than its
composition in torch exists, the composition is the entry: land it `ready`.
When the work is more than one entry, write `blocked` and name the smaller
rows in `next_question`: the session that watches the run splits the task.
